<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Miguel de Cervantes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1547">1547</a>–<a href="/years/1616">1616</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Miguel de Cervantes Saavedra</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Miguel de Cervantes was a Spanish novelist, playwright and poet, the greatest writer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-golden-age">Spanish Golden Age</a>. He fought at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lepanto">Battle of Lepanto</a> in 1571, where he lost the use of his left hand, and spent five years as a captive in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>. He later worked as a tax collector and published the two parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/don-quixote">Don Quixote</a> in 1605 and 1615, as well as the Exemplary Novels.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/miguel-de-cervantes.jpg" alt="Miguel de Cervantes" width="320" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to Juan de Jáuregui · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cervantes_J%C3%A1uregui.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Many details of Cervantes's life remain uncertain, including his name, background and appearance. He signed his name as Cerbantes, but his printers used Cervantes, which became the common form. In later life he used Saavedra, the name of a distant relative, rather than Cortinas, after his mother. It is generally accepted that he was born around 29 September 1547 in Alcalá de Henares, the second son of the barber-surgeon Rodrigo de Cervantes and Leonor de Cortinas.</p><p>Rodrigo was often in debt or looking for work, and the family moved constantly. When he was imprisoned for debt from October 1553 to April 1554, Leonor supported the family alone. They lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> until 1556, when his grandfather died. It has been suggested that one or both parents were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-christians">New Christians</a>, Catholics of Jewish ancestry, but the view has no wide following. No authenticated portrait of Cervantes is known to exist.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1569 Cervantes was forced to leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, where he worked in a cardinal's household. In 1570 he enlisted in a Spanish Navy infantry regiment. He was badly wounded at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lepanto">Battle of Lepanto</a> in October 1571. Captured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbary-coast">Barbary</a> pirates in 1575 while serving as a soldier, he was ransomed after five years and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>.</p><p>He claimed to have written more than 20 plays, such as El trato de Argel, based on his captivity, but such works were short-lived. His first significant novel, the pastoral romance La Galatea, appeared in 1585 and received little notice. He worked as a purchasing agent and later as a government tax collector. By 1605 he had not been published for 20 years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/don-quixote">Don Quixote</a>, first published in January 1605, set out explicitly to undermine vain and empty chivalric romances. Its portrayal of real life and its everyday speech were considered innovative, and it was instantly popular: Don Quixote and Sancho Panza appeared in masquerades celebrating the birth of Philip IV on 8 April 1605. An unauthorised sequel under the name Alonso Fernández de Avellaneda came out in 1614, and Cervantes published his own second part in 1615. The first part is more comic, the second more sophisticated, with greater depth of characterisation.</p><p>His other works include the twelve Novelas ejemplares of 1613, the allegorical poem Viaje del Parnaso, eight short farces, and Los trabajos de Persiles y Sigismunda, published after his death in 1617. English writers rediscovered him in the mid-18th century, and John Bowle published an annotated edition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/don-quixote">Don Quixote</a> in 1781. It has been translated into all major languages.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Miguel de Cervantes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Miguel de Cervantes</b> dramatised it in El cerco de Numancia, the siege became a theme of Spanish national identity, and the site was declared a national monument in 1882.</blockquote><a href="/empire/celtiberians">Celtiberians · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Castile's Golden Age produced <b>Miguel de Cervantes</b>, Lope de Vega, Teresa of Ávila and Diego Velázquez.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Spanish Golden Age produced <b>Miguel de Cervantes</b>’s Don Quixote (1605–1615), the plays of Lope de Vega and Pedro Calderón de la Barca, the poetry of Luis de Góngora and Francisco de Quevedo, and the paintings of El Greco, Diego Velázquez, whose Las Meninas dates from 1656, Francisco…</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Miguel de Cervantes</b> spent five years as a captive in Algiers and tried to escape four times before Trinitarian friars ransomed him in 1580.</blockquote><a href="/empire/regency-of-algiers">Regency of Algiers · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Alcalá de Henares, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.48,-3.36&amp;year=1591" aria-label="Alcalá de Henares on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4467 717 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Miguel de Cervantes"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4917 987h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4917 987h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.48,-3.36&amp;year=1591">What was here in 1591? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Idle reader: thou mayest believe me without any oath that I would this book, as it is the child of my brain, were the fairest, gayest, and cleverest that could be imagined.&#8221;</p><footer>Don Quixote, prologue to part 1 &#183; tr. John Ormsby, 1885 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/996/pg996-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…what we see there are not giants but windmills, and what seem to be their arms are the sails that turned by the wind make the millstone go.&#8221;</p><footer>Don Quixote, part 1, chapter 8 &#183; tr. John Ormsby, 1885 &#183; spoken by Sancho Panza &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/996/pg996-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…for freedom, as for honour, life may and should be ventured; and on the other hand, captivity is the greatest evil that can fall to the lot of man.&#8221;</p><footer>Don Quixote, part 2, chapter 58 &#183; tr. John Ormsby, 1885 &#183; spoken by Don Quixote &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/996/pg996-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…he became so absorbed in his books that he spent his nights from sunset to sunrise, and his days from dawn to dark, poring over them; and what with little sleep and much reading his brains got so dry that he lost his wits.&#8221;</p><footer>Don Quixote, part 1, chapter 1 &#183; tr. John Ormsby, 1885 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/996/pg996-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;If the poet be pure in his morals, he will be pure in his verses too; the pen is the tongue of the mind, and as the thought engendered there, so will be the things that it writes down.&#8221;</p><footer>Don Quixote, part 2, chapter 16 &#183; tr. John Ormsby, 1885 &#183; spoken by Don Quixote &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/996/pg996-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;As a grandmother of mine used to say, there are only two families in the world, the Haves and the Haven’ts; and she stuck to the Haves…&#8221;</p><footer>Don Quixote, part 2, chapter 20 &#183; tr. John Ormsby, 1885 &#183; spoken by Sancho Panza &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/996/pg996-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
